// SOS M5 unit 4: THE TIER WORD (sawos design 35; design 19's platform
// attribute; design 25 D-4).
//
// **THE PLATFORM ADVERTISES WHAT IT CAN DENY, AND A PROGRAM READS IT ONCE.**
// This is the whole intended use, written out: ask the System object, match on
// the answer, and decide whether to run. A program that relies on isolation
// stops here when the word is `Flat`; a program that does not carries on either
// way.
//
// THE TRANSCRIPT, read as three claims:
//
//   SOS tier: Flat            <- the platform said which tier it is
//   SOS tier: stable          <- and it says the same thing every time
//   SOS tier: kernel boundary intact   <- what `Flat` does NOT disclaim
//
// **THE THIRD LINE IS THE ONE WORTH ARGUING ABOUT.** The tier word disclaims
// exactly two things — `unmap`'s revocation half and peer isolation — and
// disclaims NOTHING about the kernel boundary. So on a flat platform a handle is
// still checked and a right is still enforced, and this program proves it the
// only way a proof of a refusal can be written: it mints a System sibling with
// the tier bit STRIPPED and asks again. The kernel refuses that ask on a flat
// platform exactly as it would on an isolated one, because rights are not
// tiered.
//
// **AND THAT REFUSAL IS A FAULT, NOT A STATUS** (design 178's faults ruling): a
// missing right is a mistake the caller could have checked, so the kernel ends
// the process rather than answering it. The program therefore prints its third
// line BEFORE the ask that kills it, and the case asserts the process fault and
// the teardown that follows — which is why this program never reaches a clean
// shutdown and has no `EXIT_OK`.

import sos.{System, ProtectionTier, SystemRight, SosStatus}

static EXIT_FAILED: UInt = 1

// EVERY System right EXCEPT `TierGet` — the keep mask that makes the sibling a
// handle which can do everything this program has been doing and cannot ask the
// one question. Spelled as the bits to KEEP rather than as an inversion,
// because a mask is monotonic and naming what survives is what a launcher
// actually writes.
static KEEP_WITHOUT_TIER: UInt32 = (SystemRight.Debug | SystemRight.Shutdown
                                    | SystemRight.ProcessSelf
                                    | SystemRight.ClockGet
                                    | SystemRight.Mint
                                    | SystemRight.Transfer)

@export("_start")
func _start(boot_handle: UInt) {
    let system = System(boot_handle: boot_handle)

    // **THE ASK.** No arguments, no resource spent, and the answer is one of
    // exactly two words.
    let tier = try system.tier() catch {
        print("SOS tier: refused: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }

    // A `match` rather than a comparison, because two words is what the enum
    // has and an exhaustive match is what makes a third word — should one ever
    // be ruled in — a compile error here rather than a silent fall-through.
    match tier {
        case Isolated -> {
            let _ = system.debug_print("SOS tier: Isolated\n")
        },
        case Flat -> {
            let _ = system.debug_print("SOS tier: Flat\n")
        },
    }

    // **A PLATFORM FACT DOES NOT CHANGE.** Asking twice is not paranoia, it is
    // the assertion that this op reads a compile-time constant rather than
    // sampling something: an implementation that answered from mutable state
    // would be a different op with the same name.
    let again = try system.tier() catch {
        print("SOS tier: second ask refused: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    if again != tier {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS tier: UNSTABLE\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS tier: stable\n")

    // **THE KERNEL BOUNDARY, ON WHATEVER TIER THIS IS.** The sibling holds every
    // System right this handle held except `TierGet`.
    let narrowed = try system.mint(rights: KEEP_WITHOUT_TIER) catch {
        print("SOS tier: mint failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }

    // Printed BEFORE the ask, because the ask does not return: a missing right
    // faults the process. This line is the last thing the program says, and the
    // kernel's fault report is the next line in the transcript.
    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS tier: kernel boundary intact\n")

    let _ = narrowed.tier()

    // Unreachable on a correct kernel — the ask above ended this process. A
    // build that gets here has answered a question through a handle that was
    // not allowed to ask it, which is a capability leak and not a tier
    // property, so it fails loudly rather than shutting down cleanly.
    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS tier: UNREACHABLE\n")
    let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
}
